From ea5be4173879cbfd5cfe62dee20ec28e07024bc7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilya Gulya <ilya@gulya.me>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 12:20:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] emulation: publish pid_for_task on the process management
 plane, never abort

pid_for_task had NO ring route and NO plane op, so the generated wrapper asked
the RETIRED per-thread RPC socket for a transport. MEASURED on prefix-verify14,
both xcodebuild workloads:

  [rpc-socket-DENIED] pid=670051 tid=670051 call=pid_for_task image=kernel delta=0x3B0A0 denied=1
  pid_for_task failed (internally): -32   (== -EPIPE, the wrapper's broken-pipe status)
  XCB_RC=134

delta=0x3B0A0 resolves against the DEPLOYED libsystem_kernel (not the server
binary: mach_driver_get_fd does not exist there) to `dserver_rpc_pid_for_task
+ 0x20`, the wrapper's up-front `dserver_rpc_hooks_get_socket()`. The core
names the caller's frames: __simple_abort <- pid_for_task_impl <-
_darling_mach_syscall <- CSCppMachTask::CSCppMachTask <-
CSSymbolicatorCreateWithTaskFlagsAndNotification <-
+[DVTStackBacktraceSymbolicator ...] <- IDEInitialize.

Two changes, both about the transport and the failure shape:

1. The process MANAGEMENT PLANE is its home, like mach_port_type (22u),
   mach_port_get_attributes (32u) and the mk_timer family (33u-36u). The
   server runs the ORDINARY Call, so the semantics exist once (directive
   section 15) -- Call::PidForTask and duct-tape's pid_for_task already exist
   and are what the datagram path used; nothing is being implemented here.
   The task name travels in payload word 0 and the OUT pid as the GUEST
   ADDRESS the ordinary Call writes through writeMemory, exactly the shape
   mach_port_get_attributes uses for its OUT parameters. Only
   `planeStatus == -1` (nothing published) falls back to the datagram.

2. A transport failure is now a kern_return_t, not a kill. pid_for_task has no
   fatal case on macOS for ANY input, and its caller here is
   CoreSymbolicationDT symbolicating an exception backtrace -- aborting inside
   it destroys the report the call exists to produce. A refused transport has
   no kern_return_t of its own, so it reports KERN_FAILURE and the caller's own
   error path runs; the refusal is not silent (mach_driver_get_fd prints
   `[rpc-socket-DENIED call=pid_for_task ...]`, the plane prints
   `[plane-refuse]`/`[plane-exhausted]`).

REUSE CHECK (before writing anything). Nothing is being invented here:
  1. THIS TREE already carries the implementation. The trap's semantics live in
     the tree's own darlingserver (duct-tape pid_for_task, and the generated
     Call::PidForTask::processCall -> dtape_pid_for_task); the guest already had
     dserver_rpc_pid_for_task. The only thing absent was a TRANSPORT, and the
     tree's own convention for exactly that is the process management plane,
     where 15 siblings of the same class already are (mach_port_type 22u,
     mach_port_get_attributes 32u, the mk_timer family 33u-36u, pthread_kill
     30u, thread_self_trap 31u).
  2. No newer upstream release of this component supplies such a route: the ring
     lane plus the process management plane is this hard fork's own perf#30
     architecture, absent from upstream Darling.
  3. FORKS (checked 2026-10-13 against the read-only checkout
     /home/ilyagulya/work/wr-fresh/vibedarling): VibeDarling/darling-xnu 5506422
     still defines `mach_driver_get_fd() { return __dserver_per_thread_socket(); }`
     -- its per-thread RPC socket is ALIVE, so the wall this patch fixes cannot
     occur there -- and its pid_for_task_impl is the pre-fix shape;
     DSERVER_PROCESS_CONTROL_OP / __dserver_plane_request / dserver_ring_op_class
     occur nowhere in the fork. There is no route to reuse, only the same
     transport this patch plugs into, which the fork does not have.
---
 .../src/xnu_syscall/mach/impl/mach_traps.c    | 38 +++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 35 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/mach/impl/mach_traps.c b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/mach/impl/mach_traps.c
index c3e0cff..db1d99b 100644
--- a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/mach/impl/mach_traps.c
+++ b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/mach/impl/mach_traps.c
@@ -1353,11 +1353,43 @@ kern_return_t pid_for_task_impl(
 				mach_port_name_t t,
 				int *x)
 {
-	int code = dserver_rpc_pid_for_task(t, x);
+	int code = -1;
+	int planeStatus = -1;
+	// perf#30 (directive section 2, dar-liaq): pid_for_task had NO ring route and NO plane op, so it fell to the
+	// retired per-thread socket. MEASURED on prefix-verify14, both xcodebuild workloads:
+	// `[rpc-socket-DENIED] pid=670051 tid=670051 call=pid_for_task image=kernel delta=0x3B0A0 denied=1` resolved
+	// against the deployed libsystem_kernel to `dserver_rpc_pid_for_task + 0x20` -- the generated wrapper's UP-FRONT
+	// `dserver_rpc_hooks_get_socket()` -- followed by `pid_for_task failed (internally): -32` and __simple_abort()
+	// from CoreSymbolicationDT's CSCppMachTask while DVTFoundation symbolicates a backtrace. The process MANAGEMENT
+	// PLANE is its home, like mach_port_type / mach_port_get_attributes / the mk_timer family already there; the
+	// server runs the ORDINARY Call, so the semantics exist once (directive section 15). The task NAME travels in
+	// payload word 0 and the OUT pid as the GUEST ADDRESS the Call writes through writeMemory -- the same shape
+	// mach_port_get_attributes uses for its OUT parameters. Only `planeStatus == -1` (nothing published) falls back
+	// to the datagram.
+	{
+		uint64_t reply0 = 0;
+		planeStatus = __dserver_plane_request_ex(DSERVER_PROCESS_CONTROL_OP_PID_FOR_TASK,
+			(uint64_t)(uint32_t)t, (uint64_t)(uintptr_t)x, 0, 0, &reply0, 0);
+		if (planeStatus != -1) {
+			// `planeStatus` IS the trap's reply code (the server publishes it as reply_status, the same value the
+			// datagram path returned), so an invalid task name keeps its own kern_return_t -- KERN_FAILURE -- and is
+			// not confused with a transport failure.
+			code = planeStatus;
+		}
+	}
+	if (planeStatus == -1) {
+		code = dserver_rpc_pid_for_task(t, x);
+	}
 
 	if (code < 0) {
-		__simple_printf("pid_for_task failed (internally): %d\n", code);
-		__simple_abort();
+		// APPLE-SHAPED FAILURE (dar-liaq). pid_for_task returns a kern_return_t and must NEVER kill its caller;
+		// Apple's man page for task_for_pid/pid_for_task has no fatal case, and the caller here is CoreSymbolication
+		// symbolicating an exception backtrace -- an abort inside it destroys the very report the call exists to
+		// produce. A transport that cannot carry the request is not the caller's fault and has no kern_return_t of
+		// its own, so it is reported as KERN_FAILURE and the caller's own error path runs. The refusal itself is not
+		// silent: mach_driver_get_fd prints `[rpc-socket-DENIED call=pid_for_task ...]` and the plane prints
+		// `[plane-refuse]/[plane-exhausted]`, so the transport state stays visible in the run log.
+		return KERN_FAILURE;
 	}
 
 	return code;
-- 
2.43.0

